94
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:26,900
The remainder of this podcast is going to be taken up by answering an email question

95
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:29,900
that came across the transom here within the past several weeks.

96
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:34,900
that, I think, raises some issues that are always worth talking about.

97
00:07:34,900 --> 00:07:37,900
So let's get started, shall we?

98
00:07:52,900 --> 00:08:00,900
When I get all steamed up, then I shout: tip me over and pull me out,

99
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:04,900
Tip me over and pull me out,

100
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:08,900
Tip me over and pull me out.

101
00:08:08,900 --> 00:08:12,900
Tip me over and pull me out.

102
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:18,900
Write me a letter, send it by name

103
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:21,900
Email. that is, folks,

104
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:24,900
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

105
00:08:31,900 --> 00:08:34,900
Well, for very obvious reasons, I'm not going to share the name

106
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:40,900
or the location of the person that asked this question via email,

107
00:08:40,900 --> 00:08:42,900
but let's just dive in.

108
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:45,900
They wrote to say: my family loves scouting,

109
00:08:45,900 --> 00:08:49,900
My son loves scouting, but we're having difficulties with our troop.

110
00:08:49,900 --> 00:08:53,900
One of our scouts got in trouble last year at school.

111
00:08:53,900 --> 00:08:56,900
He was punished at school, punished at home,

112
00:08:56,900 --> 00:09:00,900
and after the scouters of our troop found out about this incident.

113
00:09:00,900 --> 00:09:03,900
they placed him on probation for months.

114
00:09:03,900 --> 00:09:09,900
My star scout son was turned down six times for a life service project.

115
00:09:09,900 --> 00:09:11,900
The scout master told him he was too young

116
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:14,900
and too immature to be an Eagle Scout.

117
00:09:14,900 --> 00:09:18,900
The troop adopted a new system for approving star or life service projects

118
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:21,900
that requires filling out part of the Eagle paperwork

119
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:24,900
and unanimous approval of the troop committee.

120
00:09:24,900 --> 00:09:27,900
My husband and assistant scout master tried to point out

121
00:09:27,900 --> 00:09:30,900
that this was outside BSA policy,

122
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:35,900
but he was told that it has been voted as a new troop policy.

123
00:09:35,900 --> 00:09:38,900
My question is: are these policies legitimate?

124
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:41,900
Can a troop place scouts on probation for things

125
00:09:41,900 --> 00:09:43,900
that didn't even happen in scouts?

126
00:09:43,900 --> 00:09:47,900
Can a scout master prevent scouts from advancing because of their age?

127
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:50,900
These are common questions, unfortunately,

128
00:09:50,900 --> 00:09:53,900
that I've addressed a number of times

129
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:59,900
and I want to take a little time to kind of delve into them in this podcast.

130
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:01,900
The first question is:

131
00:10:01,900 --> 00:10:03,900
can a troop place scouts on probation

132
00:10:03,900 --> 00:10:05,900
for things that didn't even happen in scouts,

133
00:10:05,900 --> 00:10:08,900
Distressingly? the answer is yes.

134
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:12,900
A troop can probably adopt a policy to do that,

135
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:18,900
but to my mind it is inadvisable either to adopt the policy

136
00:10:18,900 --> 00:10:22,900
or to place scouts on probation.

137
00:10:22,900 --> 00:10:23,900
The second question:

138
00:10:23,900 --> 00:10:26,900
can a scout master prevent scouts from advancing because of their age?

139
00:10:26,900 --> 00:10:28,900
That's an easy answer: No,

140
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:33,900
Although distressingly, a lot of scout masters seem to think

141
00:10:33,900 --> 00:10:36,900
that it's their job to judge the maturity of a scout

142
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:41,900
and to put the brakes on if they feel these immature for some reason.

143
00:10:41,900 --> 00:10:44,900
The third question is the most important one, probably,

144
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:46,900
and it's not directly expressed in this email:

145
00:10:46,900 --> 00:10:51,900
How does a family deal with these kinds of things happening in a troop?

146
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:53,900
And when you get down to practicalities,

147
00:10:53,900 --> 00:10:58,900
a wrong-headed scouter can do all kinds of silly, stupid things

148
00:10:58,900 --> 00:11:01,900
that hold scouts back and you won't change their mind.

149
00:11:01,900 --> 00:11:04,900
You won't change the mind of the other adults involved.

150
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:08,900
I've worked with literally hundreds of troops and thousands of adult volunteers

151
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:13,900
and once people are entrenched in this kind of mindset

152
00:11:13,900 --> 00:11:16,900
where they're going to make policies and they're going to make judgments

153
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:21,900
about scout's maturity and they're going to hold a hard line about things,

154
00:11:21,900 --> 00:11:23,900
you're not going to get anywhere with them,

155
00:11:23,900 --> 00:11:26,900
And the advice that I've given people many, many times

156
00:11:26,900 --> 00:11:31,900
is to get out of that situation and find another troop.

157
00:11:31,900 --> 00:11:33,900
Most places there's another troop out there

158
00:11:33,900 --> 00:11:35,900
that hues a little closer to the line

159
00:11:35,900 --> 00:11:37,900
and kind of gets what scouting is all about.

160
00:11:37,900 --> 00:11:40,900
I hear about situations like this from time to time,

161
00:11:40,900 --> 00:11:43,900
and if you find yourself in this kind of situation,

162
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:47,900
the one thing that you should first understand is you're not alone,

163
00:11:47,900 --> 00:11:53,900
And the second thing is that this is not the fault of the scouts involved.

164
00:11:53,900 --> 00:11:57,900
It's the adults who are simply mistaken about their role in scouting.

165
00:11:57,900 --> 00:12:00,900
The best bet if you find yourself in that kind of situation

166
00:12:00,900 --> 00:12:02,900
is walking away from the mess and not looking back,

167
00:12:02,900 --> 00:12:06,900
not having any arguments or making any parting shots.

168
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:08,900
you just pack up and leave.

169
00:12:08,900 --> 00:12:11,900
Visit a couple of other troops, talk to the scout masters,

170
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:13,900
get your son to talk to the scout master

171
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:16,900
and you'll find one who gets the program.

172
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:18,900
So listen carefully to this, okay,

173
00:12:18,900 --> 00:12:23,900
Leave these folks who are causing you problems behind.

174
00:12:23,900 --> 00:12:26,900
No arguments, no parting shots, just leave.

175
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:30,900
The only thing that matters in this situation

176
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:32,900
is the interest of the scouts involved,

177
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:35,900
and that's the only thing that matters 100% of the time.

178
00:12:35,900 --> 00:12:39,900
In a month or two, this whole thing will be in your rear view mirror,

179
00:12:39,900 --> 00:12:42,900
Your son will be enjoying scouts and you can move on.

180
00:12:42,900 --> 00:12:46,900
That is my typical advice for that third unspoken question.

181
00:12:46,900 --> 00:12:49,900
Let's go back and examine the first two questions a little closer.

182
00:12:49,900 --> 00:12:51,900
Question number one was:

183
00:12:51,900 --> 00:12:56,900
can a troop place scouts on probation for things that didn't even happen in scouts,

184
00:12:56,900 --> 00:12:59,900
And I've had that question a couple of times in the past several weeks

185
00:12:59,900 --> 00:13:01,900
from different people.

186
00:13:01,900 --> 00:13:04,900
And just for clarity, troops are permitted

187
00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:08,900
to implement appropriate disciplinary rules and corresponding penalties

188
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:10,900
and it's mentioned in the literature.

189
00:13:10,900 --> 00:13:14,900
But my convention is that this is a very bad idea

190
00:13:14,900 --> 00:13:17,900
because we deal with every scout

191
00:13:17,900 --> 00:13:20,900
and every situation individually,

192
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:25,900
based on that scout's understanding of the scout oath and the scout law.

193
00:13:25,900 --> 00:13:29,900
If there's a discipline problem with a scout in a troop

194
00:13:29,900 --> 00:13:33,900
and it needs correction, the scout is referred to as parents

195
00:13:33,900 --> 00:13:36,900
and they can return him to active participation

196
00:13:36,900 --> 00:13:40,900
when they're ready to assure the troop that the issue is resolved.

197
00:13:40,900 --> 00:13:44,900
Scouts are not to discipline or punish children.

198
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:46,900
That's up to their parents

199
00:13:46,900 --> 00:13:48,900
And that's the way I've learned how to do things

200
00:13:48,900 --> 00:13:50,900
and what I recommend to others.

201
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:55,900
It's not necessarily a matter of official policy or procedure

202
00:13:55,900 --> 00:13:59,900
And you should always check my advice against the resources out there.

203
00:13:59,900 --> 00:14:02,900
But let me expand upon this whole idea for a moment.

204
00:14:02,900 --> 00:14:05,900
I don't think a scouter is necessarily a bad person

205
00:14:05,900 --> 00:14:08,900
or is totally misinformed

206
00:14:08,900 --> 00:14:11,900
If they decide they're going to have disciplinary rules

207
00:14:11,900 --> 00:14:14,900
and penalties and things like that in their troop.

208
00:14:14,900 --> 00:14:18,900
I just think they don't understand their role very well,

209
00:14:18,900 --> 00:14:20,900
And let me explain why.

210
00:14:20,900 --> 00:14:23,900
Our children spend the majority of their lives

211
00:14:23,900 --> 00:14:27,900
in activities and situations

212
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:30,900
where rules are made and penalties are defined,

213
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:32,900
and that is what we've come to expect.

214
00:14:32,900 --> 00:14:37,900
School districts and sports teams and community organizations

215
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:41,900
who work with young people often find themselves at an impasse

216
00:14:41,900 --> 00:14:44,900
where they have to define some rules

217
00:14:44,900 --> 00:14:47,900
and they have to define penalties for breaking those rules.

218
00:14:47,900 --> 00:14:49,900
Sounds perfectly reasonable,

219
00:14:49,900 --> 00:14:50,900
And you know what

220
00:14:50,900 --> 00:14:52,900
It is in some situations.

221
00:14:52,900 --> 00:14:56,900
But I've never seen any positive effect

222
00:14:56,900 --> 00:14:59,900
of this sort of thing in a scout troop,

223
00:14:59,900 --> 00:15:03,900
because a scout troop is a different kind of a situation.

224
00:15:03,900 --> 00:15:06,900
It's not school, it's not a sports team,

225
00:15:06,900 --> 00:15:09,900
it's not a typical activity

226
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:11,900
that a young person will involve themselves in.

227
00:15:11,900 --> 00:15:14,900
Scout troops may have some similarity with those things,

228
00:15:14,900 --> 00:15:17,900
but they are different because of the aims

229
00:15:17,900 --> 00:15:19,900
they are trying to achieve.

230
00:15:19,900 --> 00:15:22,900
The aim we're trying to achieve as scouters

231
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:24,900
is to develop character in young people

232
00:15:24,900 --> 00:15:26,900
using the scout oath and law.

233
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:28,900
We really don't have any other aims.

234
00:15:28,900 --> 00:15:31,900
There are some ancillary benefits that come out of that,

235
00:15:31,900 --> 00:15:33,900
but that is the main aim.

236
00:15:33,900 --> 00:15:35,900
That's what we're all about.

237
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:37,900
You can't develop the character

238
00:15:37,900 --> 00:15:39,900
of an entire group of people.

239
00:15:39,900 --> 00:15:42,900
You can only do it one individual at a time.

240
00:15:42,900 --> 00:15:45,900
And if you impose a bunch of rules and penalties

241
00:15:45,900 --> 00:15:47,900
for a group. what you've done

242
00:15:47,900 --> 00:15:50,900
is you've tied your hands about being able to deal

243
00:15:50,900 --> 00:15:52,900
with individual situations.

244
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:54,900
So let me explain my experience

245
00:15:54,900 --> 00:15:56,900
with a couple of situations

246
00:15:56,900 --> 00:15:58,900
that hopefully shed some light

247
00:15:58,900 --> 00:16:00,900
on what I'm trying to say.

248
00:16:00,900 --> 00:16:02,900
Many years ago one of my scouts

249
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:04,900
came to the attention of the authorities

250
00:16:04,900 --> 00:16:06,900
because he had threatened

251
00:16:06,900 --> 00:16:09,900
another young person with a pocket knife.

252
00:16:09,900 --> 00:16:12,900
In that case I did not have a whole lot of choice

253
00:16:12,900 --> 00:16:14,900
but to talk to his parents and to say

254
00:16:14,900 --> 00:16:16,900
that until that incident

255
00:16:16,900 --> 00:16:18,900
was resolved with the authorities,

256
00:16:18,900 --> 00:16:20,900
I couldn't really permit him

257
00:16:20,900 --> 00:16:23,900
to participate actively with the troop

258
00:16:23,900 --> 00:16:25,900
and that, whether or not there were

259
00:16:25,900 --> 00:16:27,900
a lot of mitigating circumstances involved,

260
00:16:27,900 --> 00:16:29,900
the incident rose to the level

261
00:16:29,900 --> 00:16:31,900
of an immediate concern

262
00:16:31,900 --> 00:16:33,900
for other people's safety

263
00:16:33,900 --> 00:16:35,900
and I had very little choice in the matter.

264
00:16:35,900 --> 00:16:38,900
I explained that I was not placing the scout

265
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:40,900
on any kind of a probation

266
00:16:40,900 --> 00:16:42,900
or trying to punish him

267
00:16:42,900 --> 00:16:44,900
in any way,

268
00:16:44,900 --> 00:16:46,900
but that the only thing

269
00:16:46,900 --> 00:16:48,900
that would satisfy the concerns

270
00:16:48,900 --> 00:16:50,900
over the safety of the other scouts

271
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:52,900
was to tell his family

272
00:16:52,900 --> 00:16:54,900
that he could not actively participate

273
00:16:54,900 --> 00:16:56,900
until these matters were resolved.

274
00:16:56,900 --> 00:16:58,900
Over the course of 30 years

275
00:16:58,900 --> 00:17:00,900
I've had scouts who've done all kinds

276
00:17:00,900 --> 00:17:02,900
of things outside of scouting

277
00:17:02,900 --> 00:17:04,900
that were inconsistent

278
00:17:04,900 --> 00:17:06,900
with the scout oath and law.

279
00:17:06,900 --> 00:17:08,900
These things have happened at school

280
00:17:08,900 --> 00:17:10,900
or in the broader community.

281
00:17:10,900 --> 00:17:12,900
They've come to the attention of

282
00:17:12,900 --> 00:17:14,900
either the school or the scout's parents

283
00:17:14,900 --> 00:17:16,900
or the scout's friends.

284
00:17:16,900 --> 00:17:18,900
I've heard about them.

285
00:17:18,900 --> 00:17:20,900
None of these things would raise

286
00:17:20,900 --> 00:17:22,900
their fellow scouts

287
00:17:22,900 --> 00:17:24,900
if they continued to participate in scouts,

288
00:17:24,900 --> 00:17:26,900
but they did raise a concern

289
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:28,900
as to how well they understood

290
00:17:28,900 --> 00:17:30,900
the scout oath and law

291
00:17:30,900 --> 00:17:32,900
and whether or not they were going to be able

292
00:17:32,900 --> 00:17:34,900
to follow the scout oath and law.

293
00:17:34,900 --> 00:17:36,900
If I become aware of one of these situations,

294
00:17:36,900 --> 00:17:38,900
I'm going to sit down

295
00:17:38,900 --> 00:17:40,900
with that individual scout

296
00:17:40,900 --> 00:17:42,900
and we're going to discuss the situation

297
00:17:42,900 --> 00:17:44,900
in the light of the scout oath and law.

298
00:17:44,900 --> 00:17:46,900
I can tell you that

299
00:17:46,900 --> 00:17:48,900
in those discussions,

300
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:50,900
if you want to say to a scout,

301
00:17:50,900 --> 00:17:52,900
your understanding of the scout oath and law

302
00:17:52,900 --> 00:17:54,900
is so imperfect

303
00:17:54,900 --> 00:17:56,900
or so radically

304
00:17:56,900 --> 00:17:58,900
outside of the norm

305
00:17:58,900 --> 00:18:00,900
that you cannot be permitted

306
00:18:00,900 --> 00:18:02,900
to be around other scouts,

307
00:18:02,900 --> 00:18:04,900
Quite to the contrary,

308
00:18:04,900 --> 00:18:06,900
what I find is that young people

309
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:08,900
are usually very, very sensitive

310
00:18:08,900 --> 00:18:10,900
to these issues

311
00:18:10,900 --> 00:18:12,900
and these ideals

312
00:18:12,900 --> 00:18:14,900
and that if you discuss it with them

313
00:18:14,900 --> 00:18:16,900
in the right manner,

314
00:18:16,900 --> 00:18:18,900
you ask them to explain

315
00:18:18,900 --> 00:18:20,900
their behavior in the light

316
00:18:20,900 --> 00:18:22,900
of the scout oath and law,

317
00:18:22,900 --> 00:18:24,900
They get it right nearly every single time

318
00:18:24,900 --> 00:18:26,900
They understand

319
00:18:26,900 --> 00:18:28,900
the difference between their behavior

320
00:18:28,900 --> 00:18:30,900
and the ideals

321
00:18:30,900 --> 00:18:32,900
that we encourage them

322
00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:34,900
to strive towards.

323
00:18:34,900 --> 00:18:36,900
Now it's not uncommon that a scout

324
00:18:36,900 --> 00:18:38,900
in this kind of situation will be dealing

325
00:18:38,900 --> 00:18:40,900
with some kind of penalty

326
00:18:40,900 --> 00:18:42,900
in another area of his life.

327
00:18:42,900 --> 00:18:44,900
He might be suspended from school,

328
00:18:44,900 --> 00:18:46,900
He might be discharged at home,

329
00:18:46,900 --> 00:18:48,900
He may have, as a result of his actions,

330
00:18:48,900 --> 00:18:50,900
had a fallout with his friends.

331
00:18:50,900 --> 00:18:52,900
But I return to the idea

332
00:18:52,900 --> 00:18:54,900
that our aim

333
00:18:54,900 --> 00:18:56,900
is to help scouts develop character,

334
00:18:56,900 --> 00:18:58,900
and the tools of developing character

335
00:18:58,900 --> 00:19:00,900
are the scout oath and law.

336
00:19:00,900 --> 00:19:02,900
and when you sit down and you discuss

337
00:19:02,900 --> 00:19:04,900
the ideals

338
00:19:04,900 --> 00:19:06,900
of the oath and law with scouts,

339
00:19:06,900 --> 00:19:08,900
rather than your judgment

340
00:19:08,900 --> 00:19:10,900
about their actions

341
00:19:10,900 --> 00:19:12,900
or their maturity

342
00:19:12,900 --> 00:19:14,900
or their actions,

343
00:19:14,900 --> 00:19:16,900
that you and the scout

344
00:19:16,900 --> 00:19:18,900
will discover a way to resolve

345
00:19:18,900 --> 00:19:20,900
the issue.

346
00:19:20,900 --> 00:19:22,900
If the only tools in your toolbox

347
00:19:22,900 --> 00:19:24,900
to resolve this kind of issue

348
00:19:24,900 --> 00:19:26,900
are a set of rules

349
00:19:26,900 --> 00:19:28,900
and policies that the troop committee

350
00:19:28,900 --> 00:19:30,900
decided they needed to vote on

351
00:19:30,900 --> 00:19:32,900
and put on pieces of paper

352
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:34,900
that also involve penalties,

353
00:19:34,900 --> 00:19:36,900
you're not going to be able to resolve

354
00:19:36,900 --> 00:19:38,900
the issue with the scout.

355
00:19:38,900 --> 00:19:40,900
You're not going to have the opportunity

356
00:19:40,900 --> 00:19:42,900
or the values of the scout oath and law

357
00:19:42,900 --> 00:19:44,900
within the scout. You're only going to be able

358
00:19:44,900 --> 00:19:46,900
to issue a judgment and a punishment.

359
00:19:46,900 --> 00:19:48,900
So consider, before

360
00:19:48,900 --> 00:19:50,900
trying to set out a list of rules

361
00:19:50,900 --> 00:19:52,900
with associated penalties,

362
00:19:52,900 --> 00:19:54,900
consider how much you are

363
00:19:54,900 --> 00:19:56,900
limiting yourself in your work

364
00:19:56,900 --> 00:19:58,900
as a scouter. Now the second question

365
00:19:58,900 --> 00:20:00,900
here: can

366
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:02,900
a scout master prevent scouts

367
00:20:02,900 --> 00:20:04,900
from advancing because of their age?

368
00:20:04,900 --> 00:20:06,900
The short and long answers

369
00:20:06,900 --> 00:20:08,900
are both yes and no.

370
00:20:08,900 --> 00:20:10,900
If you go through the Guide to Advancement,

371
00:20:10,900 --> 00:20:12,900
you will not find

372
00:20:12,900 --> 00:20:14,900
a statement specifically

373
00:20:14,900 --> 00:20:16,900
prohibiting this idea,

374
00:20:16,900 --> 00:20:18,900
because the Guide to Advancement

375
00:20:18,900 --> 00:20:20,900
can't deal with every

376
00:20:20,900 --> 00:20:22,900
stupid idea

377
00:20:22,900 --> 00:20:24,900
that somebody may have about

378
00:20:24,900 --> 00:20:26,900
who gets to advance. when

379
00:20:26,900 --> 00:20:28,900
The Guide to Advancement isn't cursing the darkness,

380
00:20:28,900 --> 00:20:30,900
it's lighting a candle. okay,

381
00:20:30,900 --> 00:20:32,900
And if you go and you read the Guide to Advancement

382
00:20:32,900 --> 00:20:34,900
advising us on how to

383
00:20:34,900 --> 00:20:36,900
judge the suitability of a scout

384
00:20:36,900 --> 00:20:38,900
for a given award, you won't see

385
00:20:38,900 --> 00:20:40,900
any ages mentioned,

386
00:20:40,900 --> 00:20:42,900
other than the fact that

387
00:20:42,900 --> 00:20:44,900
there's a minimum age for

388
00:20:44,900 --> 00:20:46,900
becoming a scout and a maximum age

389
00:20:46,900 --> 00:20:48,900
for staying a scout. To expand on that

390
00:20:48,900 --> 00:20:50,900
question, I can understand

391
00:20:50,900 --> 00:20:52,900
why a scouter would look at

392
00:20:52,900 --> 00:20:54,900
a younger scout challenging

393
00:20:54,900 --> 00:20:56,900
one of the higher ranks-

394
00:20:56,900 --> 00:20:58,900
Star, Life or Eagle- and be concerned

395
00:20:58,900 --> 00:21:00,900
about the scout's relative

396
00:21:00,900 --> 00:21:02,900
maturity. But maturity

397
00:21:02,900 --> 00:21:04,900
is not mentioned

398
00:21:04,900 --> 00:21:06,900
explicitly in any requirement

399
00:21:06,900 --> 00:21:08,900
for any of those ranks.

400
00:21:08,900 --> 00:21:10,900
Some of the requirements may indeed

401
00:21:10,900 --> 00:21:12,900
describe the aspects

402
00:21:12,900 --> 00:21:14,900
of a person's character that would indicate

403
00:21:14,900 --> 00:21:16,900
maturity, but there's no exact

404
00:21:16,900 --> 00:21:18,900
measure of maturity mentioned anywhere

405
00:21:18,900 --> 00:21:20,900
in those requirements. And one rabbit

406
00:21:20,900 --> 00:21:22,900
hole that we can jump down for

407
00:21:22,900 --> 00:21:24,900
just a moment is the

408
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:26,900
whole discussion that comes up

409
00:21:26,900 --> 00:21:28,900
when there's a 13 or 14 year old

410
00:21:28,900 --> 00:21:30,900
Eagle scout, and everybody

411
00:21:30,900 --> 00:21:32,900
tears out their hair

412
00:21:32,900 --> 00:21:34,900
and wrens their garments over the fact

413
00:21:34,900 --> 00:21:36,900
that this is an impossible thing.

414
00:21:36,900 --> 00:21:38,900
that should never happen, and

415
00:21:38,900 --> 00:21:40,900
I will get out of the rabbit hole

416
00:21:40,900 --> 00:21:42,900
by saying this: there is only

417
00:21:42,900 --> 00:21:44,900
one way to become

418
00:21:44,900 --> 00:21:46,900
an Eagle scout, And that is

419
00:21:46,900 --> 00:21:48,900
to fulfill the requirements

420
00:21:48,900 --> 00:21:50,900
and pass a duly

421
00:21:50,900 --> 00:21:52,900
constituted border review.

422
00:21:52,900 --> 00:21:54,900
That's it. There's no other

423
00:21:54,900 --> 00:21:56,900
way to become an Eagle scout. Now you

424
00:21:56,900 --> 00:21:58,900
can argue until the cows come

425
00:21:58,900 --> 00:22:00,900
home about exactly what an Eagle

426
00:22:00,900 --> 00:22:02,900
scout is and how it should be

427
00:22:02,900 --> 00:22:04,900
and everything like that. The most

428
00:22:04,900 --> 00:22:06,900
satisfactory answer I have to those

429
00:22:06,900 --> 00:22:08,900
arguments is that becoming

430
00:22:08,900 --> 00:22:10,900
an Eagle scout is certainly achieving

431
00:22:10,900 --> 00:22:12,900
a rank by completing

432
00:22:12,900 --> 00:22:14,900
requirements, but it is only

433
00:22:14,900 --> 00:22:16,900
the beginning of a process

434
00:22:16,900 --> 00:22:18,900
that carries on throughout

435
00:22:18,900 --> 00:22:20,900
one's life, And if we've done

436
00:22:20,900 --> 00:22:22,900
our job as scouters, we

437
00:22:22,900 --> 00:22:24,900
understand our job as scouters

438
00:22:24,900 --> 00:22:26,900
as achieving the aim of

439
00:22:26,900 --> 00:22:28,900
developing character. through the scout

440
00:22:28,900 --> 00:22:30,900
law, All of our scouts,

441
00:22:30,900 --> 00:22:32,900
no matter what rank they achieve,

442
00:22:32,900 --> 00:22:34,900
no matter how many advancement

443
00:22:34,900 --> 00:22:36,900
awards they have on their shirts

444
00:22:36,900 --> 00:22:38,900
when they leave us have

445
00:22:38,900 --> 00:22:40,900
tools to become kind,

446
00:22:40,900 --> 00:22:42,900
compassionate, contributing citizens,

447
00:22:42,900 --> 00:22:44,900
strong, inspiring

448
00:22:44,900 --> 00:22:46,900
leaders and all around

449
00:22:46,900 --> 00:22:48,900
good folks for the rest

450
00:22:48,900 --> 00:22:50,900
of their lives. That's what's really

451
00:22:50,900 --> 00:22:52,900
important, isn't it? So that's

452
00:22:52,900 --> 00:22:54,900
a very long answer

453
00:22:54,900 --> 00:22:56,900
to a relatively

454
00:22:56,900 --> 00:22:58,900
simple, multi-question

455
00:22:58,900 --> 00:23:00,900
email,

